<urn:atm:triage:finding/AW-POOL-V9-ARCH-002>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "AW-POOL-V9-ARCH-002" ;
  triage:title "FIX-10's doctor lease-lookup budget only gates between loop iterations, not the in-flight lookup call itself" ;
  triage:description "quality-mgr's PR #1244 (A2, fix/aw-pool-read-migration) re-QA (arch-qa reviewer): crates/atm-core/src/doctor/mod.rs:459 LEASE_LOOKUP_BUDGET (Duration::from_secs(2)) is checked only between iterations of the roster .filter_map() loop, not as a deadline on the in-flight runtime.graft_receiver_lease(...) call itself (line ~469). A single slow/saturated lookup can still consume up to the ReaderPool's full request_deadline (10s) before the loop re-checks elapsed time, so the whole operation's real wall-clock bound is ~10s worst case for one call, not the intended 2s total. This does fix the original O(N)-lookups-times-10s starvation (the per-iteration early-exit loop-bypass is closed and correctly implemented), but does not truly bound the whole operation to 2s as FIX-10's acceptance criteria (constant-budget across the whole per-member loop) claims. Fix direction: thread a per-call deadline (derived from the remaining budget: LEASE_LOOKUP_BUDGET minus lookup_started.elapsed()) into the lease lookup call itself, not just a gate on whether the next iteration starts." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-aw" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "rust-service-hardening" ;
  triage:severity "important" ;
  triage:repeatable false ;
  triage:sweepScope "file_only" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-09-05T02:30:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
